In this problem set, I learned how to model heat transfer through a multilayer photovoltaic (PV) panel using a thermal resistance network. The panel consisted of glass, an adhesive layer, and a silicon layer, each contributing different conductive resistances. I had to account for how solar irradiation interacts with the panel, including portions that are reflected, absorbed at the surface, and transmitted into the silicon. By setting up the resistance network, I analyzed how heat flows from the silicon through the adhesive and glass to the surrounding air, while also considering convective and radiative heat losses at the outer surface.

I also learned how energy conversion within the silicon affects the thermal analysis. Only part of the absorbed solar energy becomes electricity, while the rest becomes thermal energy that raises the silicon temperature. Because the solar-to-electric conversion efficiency decreases as the silicon temperature increases, the problem required coupling the heat transfer analysis with the efficiency equation. This helped me understand how thermal conditions directly influence PV performance and how conduction, convection, radiation, and contact resistance all impact the operating temperature of the panel.



